WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:18.790
Hi, everyone. My name is Madison, and I am a rising junior studying computer science at Columbia University, and this summer I am a tech sales solutions specialist intern here at IBM.

2
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:25.790
Okay, so let's get into describing some common Unix shell commands. First up, ID.

3
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:33.790
The ID command has no options. It displays the UID, the username, the GID, and the group name that are assigned to the user.

4
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:39.790
If a user is a member of other supplemental groups, they are listed at the end of the output.

5
00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:45.790
The date command produces the time and the date in a predefined format, depending on locale.

6
00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:53.790
There are many options for the date command, which can be used to change the format and produce different parts of the time and date.

7
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:59.790
The ENV command displays the list of currently assigned environment variables and their current values.

8
00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:08.790
There is also a command called set, which, when entered without options, displays all of the currently assigned shell variables and their values.

9
00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:15.790
The environment variables are a subset of the shell variables, so set should normally produce a longer list than ENV.

10
00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:21.790
The type command displays information about the command that is entered following type.

11
00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:29.790
Commands can be built in, aliases, or utility programs that must be loaded and executed at invocation.

12
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.790
The type command tells you which command is which.

13
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:39.790
If a command is actually a utility program, type displays the path name of the program.

14
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:47.790
The type command can also tell you that the program has been cached, which means that it has been copied into storage for performance reasons.

15
00:01:48.790 --> 00:02:00.790
The file system hierarchy, which consists of all directories and all files available to the Unix environment, is actually made up of many file systems interconnected together at various mount points.

16
00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:09.790
In order for a file system to be usable by ZOS Unix and to be connected to the file system hierarchy, the file system must be mounted.

17
00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:12.790
Mounting a file system does two things.

18
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:17.790
First, the file system is allocated to Unix kernel's address space.

19
00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.790
Second, the file system is connected into the hierarchy at its defined mount point.

20
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:28.790
The mount point is defined by the command, statement, or function that mounts the file system.

21
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.790
A mount point must be a directory.

22
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:39.790
There are several ways to find out which file systems are currently mounted and available for use by ZOS Unix.

23
00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:48.790
The ZOS operator command can be used to display a list of mounted file systems at the operator console and in the system log.

24
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:54.790
This command can be entered from a system console or from the SDSF log screen.

25
00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:59.790
Another way to display the mounted file systems is to use the iShell.

26
00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.790
From the iShell main menu, go to the file underscore systems action bar item.

27
00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.790
Press enter to pull down the file underscore systems menu.

28
00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.790
Select the mount table option to display the table of mounted file systems.

29
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.790
Any ZOS Unix user is allowed to do this.

30
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:26.790
The third method to display the mounted file systems is to use the ZOS Unix Shell DF command, which stands for display file systems.

31
00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:32.790
Here is an example of output in SDSF from the DOMBSF command.

32
00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.790
This displays information about the specified file systems.

33
00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.790
One wildcard is permitted in the name provided.

34
00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:45.790
Here is an example of output from iShell mount table showing all of our available file systems.

35
00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:55.790
Historically, some functions related to Unix file systems have been provided by the options available on the file underscore systems pull down menu

36
00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.790
in the Unix system services ISPF shell.

37
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:07.790
At the same time, other functions related to Unix file systems have been provided by the ZOS Unix directory list utility,

38
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.790
which is accessed by using ISPF option 3.17.

39
00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:18.790
In ZOS, the 2.2 functions that were previously available within the Unix system services ISPF shell

40
00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:23.790
are now also available by using ISPF option 3.17.

41
00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:29.790
With this change, these functions are combined with other Unix functions available in option 3.17.

42
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:38.790
When there are a large number of mounted Unix file systems, the file system list presented by the Unix system services ISPF shell can become unmanageable.

43
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:46.790
So you might be better accessing the new file system functions by using the file underscore systems pull down menu instead.

44
00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:52.790
The DF command displays the currently mounted and available file systems in your ZOS Unix environment.

45
00:04:52.790 --> 00:05:00.790
It also displays the size of the file system in blocks and the number of blocks that are available for use in each file system.

46
00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:10.790
There is an option for DF-P, note that it is an uppercase P, which produces output that is a little different than the default DF option.

47
00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:16.790
With the P option, the output also displays the percentage of the file system that is full.

48
00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:21.790
You can ask the DF command to display information about only one file system.

49
00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:30.790
If you do not include the name of a file system on the DF command, the default is to display information about all mounted file systems.

50
00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:40.790
Here is an example of output from the DF command showing the currently mounted and available file systems in a ZOS Unix environment.

51
00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:46.790
The FSINFO function is used to obtain detailed file system information.

52
00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:57.790
FSINFO provides more detailed information for single, multiple file systems in a faster and more flexible manner, including Cisplex-wide information.

53
00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:04.790
In the next video, we'll take a look at some more commonly used shell commands, starting with those related to the current working directory.
